{"causes":["Aeacides was born into a prominent family and received a traditional Greek education, which prepared him for a life of politics and leadership.","The Achaean League's dominance in Epirus created an environment of instability that Aeacides sought to address through his military campaigns.","The Roman Republic's growing interest in the Mediterranean region also played a role in shaping Aeacides' foreign policy."],"effects":["Aeacides' alliance with the Romans led to the eventual fall of the Achaean League and the rise of Roman power in Epirus.","His military campaigns expanded Epirus' borders and created new trade routes, which boosted the region's economy.","The establishment of Pydase as a major city marked the beginning of a new era of Greek urbanization and cultural development."],"relationships":[{"sourceSlug":"Achaean League","sourceName":"Achaean League","verb":"TRANSFORMS","targetSlug":"aeacides-of-epirus","targetName":"Aeacides of Epirus","context":"During the Battle of Leucas, Aeacides led his army to victory against the Achaean League."},{"sourceSlug":"Roman Republic","sourceName":"Roman Republic","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"aeacides-of-epirus","targetName":"Aeacides of Epirus","context":"Aeacides' alliance with the Romans led to the eventual fall of the Achaean League and the rise of Roman power in Epirus."},{"sourceSlug":"Pydase","sourceName":"Pydase","verb":"CREATES","targetSlug":"aeacides-of-epirus","targetName":"Aeacides of Epirus","context":"The city of Pydase was built during Aeacides' reign and became a major center of culture and learning."},{"sourceSlug":"Alexander the Great","sourceName":"Alexander the Great","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"aeacides-of-epirus","targetName":"Aeacides of Epirus","context":"Aeacides' military campaigns were likely influenced by Alexander's own conquests in Asia Minor."},{"sourceSlug":"King Alcetas I","sourceName":"King Alcetas I","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"aeacides-of-epirus","targetName":"Aeacides of Epirus","context":"Aeacides' claim to the throne was based on his descent from King Alcetas I."},{"sourceSlug":"Epirus","sourceName":"Epirus","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"aeacides-of-epirus","targetName":"Aeacides of Epirus","context":"Aeacides' reign marked the beginning of a new era in Epirus, with significant cultural and economic developments."},{"sourceSlug":"Leucas","sourceName":"Leucas","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"aeacides-of-epirus","targetName":"Aeacides of Epirus","context":"The Battle of Leucas was a significant victory for Aeacides against the Achaean League."},{"sourceSlug":"Battle of Pydase","sourceName":"Battle of Pydase","verb":"INFLUENCES","targetSlug":"aeacides-of-epirus","targetName":"Aeacides of Epirus","context":"Aeacides died in battle at the Battle of Pydase."}],"places":[{"name":"Pydase","role":"Battle site"}],"texts":[{"title":"Wikipedia: Aeacides of Epirus","type":"Reference article","slug":"aeacides-of-epirus"}],"externalLinks":["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q403377","https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Aeacides_of_Epirus"],"tags":["Monarchs & Rulers","political-systems","empire-and-colonialism","geopolitical-linkage"],"thumbnailUrl":"","quote":"","legacySummary":"","summary":"Aeacides of Epirus (died 312 BCE) was King of Epirus in northwestern Greece (modern Albania/northwestern Greece) — father of Pyrrhus of Epirus, one of antiquity's greatest generals, and a significant figure in the turbulent Successor (Diadochi) politics that followed Alexander the Great's death in 323 BCE. Aeacides ruled Epirus from approximately 330 BCE until his death in 312 BCE, navigating the complex and shifting alliances of the early Diadochi period that followed Alexander's death and attempting to maintain Epirote independence and power amid the competing claims of Cassander, Antigonus, and other successors.

Aeacides was a member of the Aeacid dynasty — the royal house of Epirus that traced its lineage back to Achilles's son Neoptolemus (and thus to Aeacus, the father of Achilles), making them, in Greek royal genealogical tradition, distant kin of Alexander the Great (whose mother Olympias was an Epirote princess of the same Aeacid dynasty). This genealogical connection — and the fact that Olympias herself sought refuge in Epirus — drew Aeacides into the Macedonian succession struggles. He was a supporter of Olympias and the claims of Alexander IV (Alexander the Great's posthumous son by Roxane) against Cassander, who had seized effective control of Macedonia.

Aeacides's support for Olympias brought him into direct military conflict with Cassander — and the Epirote people, exhausted by the costs of these Macedonian political adventures, revolted against him, expelling Aeacides and replacing him with a different ruler. Aeacides fought to regain his kingdom and was killed in battle in 312 BCE, leaving his young son Pyrrhus to eventually recover the Epirote throne and build the extraordinary military career that would make Epirus temporarily one of the major powers of the Mediterranean world.","subjects":["Hellenistic History","Epirus","Classical Era","Ancient Greece","Diadochi","Ancient History","Greek Kingdoms","Classical Greece"],"frameworks":["STRUCTURAL_ANALYSIS","CAUSE_AND_EFFECT"],"historicalSignificance":{"significanceScore":7,"significanceNarrative":"Aeacides' reign marked a significant turning point in the history of Epirus, as he transformed the region from a small Achaean League state to a major power under Roman influence.","significanceCategory":"regional"},"_editLog":[]}
